[A one-off exploration initiated by the owner; the human goal follows below. This task has exploration_task_id.]
First use desktop_pet_exploration_task list/get to consult relevant resources and experiences; desktop_pet_schema list can retrieve legacy candidate workflows. Form a few hypotheses from available tool declarations, run minimal experiments, and adapt to evidence. Use progress at the start and after meaningful steps, reporting actual actions rather than plans or equating a successful call with goal completion.
Store concise structured products in two categories through desktop_pet_exploration_task:
1. resources: title, kind (text/image/audio/video/file), summary, content (concise text), source (actual origin), location (actual resource address). Text requires content; media/files require a real address. Never invent URLs or describe undownloaded resources as local files. Maximum 1200 characters for content, 240 for summary, 12 resources per task. Deduplicate and retain only relevant information.
2. experiences: title, conditions (applicability and limits), path (actual steps, at most six, each at most 160 characters), outcome (success/partial/failed), result (actual outcome and key evidence), feedback (observations or lessons), feedback_source (observation/user/unverified). Result and feedback each allow 300 characters; at most 8 experiences per task. Failed experiences are useful. Never invent user feedback or record lengthy private reasoning.
Each products submission contains the complete resources and experiences snapshot, replacing existing products for this task. Call get before updating and preserve useful existing entries. Use progress to save partial discoveries. To complete, submit products and a result overview of at most 400 characters. Empty categories should be empty arrays; do not force findings. If unable to finish, use fail with the reason and any partial products. Chat replies do not replace persistent reports; never attribute unrelated chats to this task.
Aim to test at most three hypotheses and stop when the goal is achieved or no worthwhile direction remains. Do not schedule recurring wakes; this is guidance, not an enforced call budget. Respect existing permissions and ask before external sending, deletion or changes not authorized by the goal. Recheck conditions and permissions before reusing experience.
Finish with a brief chat report of resource and experience counts, key findings and unresolved questions.

